Quoting  Rast  <rast2@hotmail.com>:
>As http://slackwell.com/nethack/magresponse.txt puts it, "The list of 
>requirements for the quest is extremely strange, because the requirements 
>vary wildly from quest to quest."

Also available at
<http://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~damerell/games/magresponse.txt>
since I wrote it.

Of course that's a response to MAG r1, but many of the deficiencies remain
uncorrected. Focus on first ascensions is still bad; first ascensions tend
to be very close affairs. People who want to ascend should learn from the
ascensions of experienced players who leave as little as possible to
chance.

Some of the bad advice is now thoroughly ingrained. It's unfortunate that
the author handles criticism by emphasising the bad advice and trying to
rationalise continuing to provide it. The prime example is;

"You generally do not want to come back after entering Gehennom. I got a
lot of criticism for saying that, but I am trying to emphasize the need for
thorough preparation at this point."

There's a lot of criticism for saying that _because it's wrong_. You _do_
want to come back after entering Gehennom - there's lots of lovely Stuff
there to be hauled back to your primary stash, you will certainly want to
do a stash visit before killing Rodney (and probably also distribute care
packages to DL 1 etc), and there is little particular need for preparation;
the first maze level in Gehennom isn't somehow magic compared with the
maze levels that very likely appeared below Medusa, other than the
inability to pray (which must already be contended with in the Valley of
the Dead - and note, in context here, the Valley is not being counted as
part of Gehennom).

"Get your gear fixed up, check that you have everything you need to
survive (and to do the invocation if you don't plan to come back)"

Better advice would be, if you don't plan to come back, slap yourself with
a kipper until you do. Elsewhere the MAG is sensible about stashes. I know
speed-ascenders won't be, but that's not the idea here.

That list of requirements for "the Quest" is still cluttering up the top
of the document and it's still silly. Lord Surtur is not Master Kaen and
should not be treated as such.

Not determining BUC status with altars to avoid minor stacking problems in
classes of items that generally should be bagged or stashed anyway is
demented.

The list of starting strategies now has three largely similar ones;
conservative, explorer, and balanced. Distinguishing them is hard; that's
because they started as a set of recommendations about XL relative to DL,
which are rarely of any use. They should be stripped. There are two
fundamental distinctions here; pacifist/protectioneer versus non-pacifist,
and the degree to which a character will let the pet fight, incurring
danger now to avoid danger from tough monsters generated by high XL later.
Those are the distinctions which should be discussed.

Five first ascensions is still a meaningless sample.

"Tengu" is still pluralised thusly.

The danger level of water crossing methods should be mentioned. So should
the relative utility of cursed and confused gold detection. The incredible
utility of level teleport is not emphasised - I have a more general
problem with the MAG here that, although it purports to be a strategy
guide, it's very coy about actually recommending strategy.

Negative AC does also sometimes reduce the probability of a hit as
well as the damage, and yes, I am quoting magresponse directly here.

MC 3 is still not almost essential even if it is in scare quotes, and a
cloak of displacement doesn't just work early in the game (and I'd like to
see you get the attribute another way...)

The list of AC combinations is still pointless. You've got it or you
haven't.

The Sanctum section should mention the stylish entrance via levelport;
should not encourage chopping up the entire mob in the graveyard; should
- and, again, the strategy guide won't recommend strategy - emphasise the
need to move quickly once Rodney is dead.
